# The hook is a file a shell executes, not a file a person reads, so its bytes
# are pinned here rather than left to a clone's local configuration.
#
# What is measured. git rewrites the hook's line endings on the way into a
# working tree on at least one platform, so the file the shell is handed is not
# the file that was committed. On x86_64-pc-windows-msvc with core.autocrlf true,
# before this file existed:
#
#     git --work-tree=<elsewhere> checkout-index -f -- .githooks/pre-push
#     od -c <elsewhere>/.githooks/pre-push | head -1
#     0000000   #   !   /   b   i   n   /   s   h  \r  \n   #       R   u   n
#
# so the first line arrived as `#!/bin/sh` followed by a carriage return.
#
# What is not measured, and is the reason rather than the evidence. A shell that
# reads that line looks for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return
# and refuses the hook, with a message that points at the interpreter rather than
# at the byte. That was NOT reproduced here: the shell on the machine this was
# written on ran a script with that shebang without complaint. The shell git
# hands a hook to is not the same program everywhere, and the failure is one
# nobody would read correctly, so the bytes are pinned rather than the behaviour
# assumed to hold.
#
# This pins everything under that directory rather than one file, because the
# next hook has the same first line and a rule that has to be remembered per file
# is one that will be forgotten.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Fixtures, and the cases that prove the fixture policy bites.
#
# A fixture exists to show a parser the bytes an upstream actually produces, so a
# fixture that was rewritten on the way into a working tree is showing it
# something else. `docs/decisions/fixtures.md` states that rule and
# `tests/fixture_policy.rs` refuses a `raw` fixture holding a byte that does not
# survive the trip.
#
# That check found this. Its own cases are `raw` and were written by hand, so
# they had never made the round trip; the first checkout after they landed gave
# them a carriage return each and the check refused them, on one platform and not
# on the other:
#
#     od -c tests/fixture_policy/cases/an_extract_with_no_licence/fixtures/invented_lines.txt | tail -2
#     0000120   0   .   9   3           3   7   1   6   2   .   7   5  \r  \n
#
# against `\n` in the index. So the bytes are pinned here, which is the same
# repair as the one above and is the policy applied to its own evidence.
#
# What this does not do is make a carriage return storable in a raw fixture. It
# makes `Encoding: raw` mean the same thing on every clone, and a fixture that
# genuinely needs that byte is still one that belongs in `base64`, which is what
# the policy says and what the check refuses on.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf
tests/fixture_policy/cases/** text eol=lf
